{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi3x1bz5Dv3hoDjQYiXG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Thugby Turbo** is built as a single file in this folder's root, with all four sections in the About screen and in `exit-interview.json` (parses as JSON, every field filled). The one command `node test/all.mjs` runs four suites, and all 123 checks pass. They cover the headless sim tests, the browser checks, a scripted playthrough of all four cup rounds, and the AI Hub harness in three runs. I have not played it by hand on a real pad or TV.\n\n- **Delivered:** `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-070002.html`, 1.4 MB, with the dev files in the matching subfolder and `build.mjs` to rebuild it.\n- **Departures from PLAN.md:**\n  - The AI Hub mode is parallel (each player plays their own match against the CPU, ranked by points margin), not the shared-world mode the plan describes.\n  - The engine is unmodified, so the plan's four engine patches were not made. The controller glyphs are drawn with pixels instead.\n  - Scrums, lineouts, penalties and goal kicks are all in, as simplified mini-games with HUD overlays rather than the plan's separate behind-the-kicker camera.\n- **Modes and features:** Mega Cup (four story matches), Exhibition (1 to 2 players, co-op or versus), Training Ground (nine drills), Records with initials, Settings (including the Xbox glyph option), About, Dev Mode from the pause menu with live sliders and reset to defaults, attract mode after 10 seconds idle, and the touch-only \"Controller or keyboard required\" screen.\n- **Not checked:** the mouse pass-and-aim controls were only partly exercised, and a real second controller was never used. The Xbox glyphs were screenshotted but not verified against real hardware.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:00:02\n- **Source:** rugby (over the top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (Thugby Turbo, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort)\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade sports (rugby sevens, NBA Jam-style turbo). TV broadcast camera at a 40 degree pitch, switchable to a 55 degree three-quarter and a top-down view.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (Canvas 2D, Web Audio), AI Hub Session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, no other libraries, engine unmodified\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native HD pixel-art skeleton characters in team kits, procedural striped pitch with mud, stands with animated crowd, goalposts, sin-bin dumpster, venue weather and lights\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig and E.MOVES, with new moves added for rugby (tackle, big hit, pass, kick, fend, spin, sidestep) and poses for ruck, scrum, lineout lift, celebration and launched bodies\n- **Audio:** all music, effects and voices written in code, with layered match music, whistles, hooter, crowd bed, hits and Animalese-style babble voices\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, parallel mode; couch play for 1 to 2 players\n- **Testing:** headless simulation tests, scripted AI-vs-AI matches in all four venues, a scripted browser playthrough, headless Chromium checks and the AI Hub harness; no subagents\n- **Research:** PLAN.md, the engine README and API card plus engine source for Input.halfKeys, TileMap, Settings and audio, the AI Hub README, retrofit and session client; no web search\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About eight:\n  - a stale phase handler running after a mid-step phase change (a crash)\n  - CPU set pieces waiting for scripted seats (an attract-mode stall)\n  - the kick-off handed to the wrong player\n  - invisible goalposts\n  - missing font glyphs for arrows and ticks\n  - an over-frequent interception and fumble rate\n  - a lost-keypress test bug\n  - a test helper that left the ball in the kick-off\n- **How many were critical?** Two: the phase-change crash (a try scored during the kick-off ran the kick-off handler against a try state) and the attract-mode stall.\n- **Most helpful test:** full AI-vs-AI matches headless in all four venues, with a watchdog counter that fails on any stuck phase, plus the table of direct scenario checks (tackle to ruck, monster hit, try to conversion to kick-off, scrum, lineout, penalty, cards, hazards).\n- **Least helpful test:** the AI Hub harness runs. They confirmed protocol compliance but passed first time and caught nothing. The screenshot sweeps only caught look problems a human would also see.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** characters that are data-driven 3D skeletons, where a new animation is one E.MOVES entry. The pause, settings, controls and rebinding screens come free, and the fixed-step `game.advance` made headless testing easy.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:**\n  - The pixel font has no arrow, tick or controller glyphs, so I drew badges with pixels.\n  - `px.line` with a width did not show for goalposts, but rects did.\n  - `Input.halfKeys` hands out buttons in preset order.\n  - The pointer coordinate space took a guess.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** the whole match simulation (phases, laws, set pieces, referee sight, ball physics with the oval bounce, CPU brains), plus a 10-foot menu system and glyph badges.\n- **Hardest part:** fitting a rules state machine with many mini-games onto per-tick input edges and depth-sorted drawing. Every phase handler had to survive the phase changing mid-step.\n- **Engine changes:** none. The game adds its own broadcast view and E.MOVES entries from outside.\n- **One change I'd make:** add controller button and arrow glyphs to the font, and a team-sports phase kit (restarts, set pieces, a referee).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the numbered plan with every rule and number written down, which turned design arguments into lookups. The one-command, failures-only testing instruction was also clear.\n- **Confusing:**\n  - The plan names different files and effort (opus max, 20261103) from the task (sonnet medium, 20261104), so I followed TASK.md.\n  - The plan assumes a shared-world hub session, while the task only says to follow the hub library.\n  - The plan is sized well beyond a medium-effort build.\n- **Hardest thing:** fitting a max-effort plan into one medium session and choosing what to simplify while keeping every listed feature present.\n- **What I'd change:** tier the plan into must, should and could; give the file-name convention once; say which hub mode is expected; say where usage numbers come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":795,"cache_read_input_tokens":370903,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":795},"output_tokens":767,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3e580ef-9188-4ffd-962e-28d451837174","uuid":"8e2dbc58-a034-41e4-96b5-871f86529691","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:33:08.408Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi3x1CvXzPSJE1KGJNSi"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Several conditions are unmet on transcript evidence. (1) The required file name was not used: STAGE.md asked for ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-070002.html, which matches, so that part is fine, but the Engine Feedback is later contradicted by the 'Stuck or guessed wrong' and 'Engine changes' answers only loosely. (2) TASK.md requires every feature with no placeholders or stubs, yet the transcript shows the build's own departures: scrums, lineouts, penalties and goal kicks are 'simplified mini-games' rather than the plan's specified behind-the-kicker camera, the mouse controls were 'only partly exercised', and the Xbox glyphs were 'not verified', and the build writes empty placeholder hooks such as the boot steps 'Pumping up the ball' with empty functions. (3) The exit-interview.json was generated by a script from docs/answers.mjs, and its 'usage' field is 'unknown', which is permitted, but the testingFeedback and engineFeedback text was written from an aggregated answer file rather than answered from the session. (4) The final reply does contain all four required sections. Overall the file exists and the JSON parses, but feature completeness against TASK.md (no stubs, every feature implemented) is not established by the transcript, and the reply itself admits untested features."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3e580ef-9188-4ffd-962e-28d451837174","uuid":"4945e5b8-8b23-43f7-b1a0-238317dfb56f","timestamp":"2026-1